// @no-instantiate found struct.get of type i32
// ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. A struct field whose type is a literal union is stored as the
// i32 ATOM, and pushing it into a `string[]` never widens the atom back to a string. The
// module fails validation and `vl check --codegen` exits 0. The engine names the source
// exactly — `local.set expected type (ref 2), found struct.get of type i32` — and the
// `struct.get` in that message is the field read, so the gap is at the field boundary.
//
// THE FIELD IS THE WHOLE DEFECT. The same value in any other binding class widens correctly,
// which is what says this is rep plumbing rather than a missing conversion in general:
//
//   const v: K = "ka"              ; xs.push(v)     // OK — a LOCAL
//   function f(v: K) { xs.push(v) }                 // OK — a PARAMETER
//   const r: R = { f: "ka" }       ; xs.push(r.f)   // MISCOMPILES — this file
//
// The gap is also PER-SINK, not blanket. Two sinks in this family are check-clean invalid
// wasm — this one and the `string | null` return pinned by its sibling — while a map STORE is
// a loud type error, and a plain `string` return and `"v=" + r.f` both work. So the widening
// exists on some paths and is missing on others, which is why the two broken sinks are pinned
// as two files: fixing one need not fix the other.
//
// WHY IT IS PINNED HERE AND NOT FIXED. It is the PREREQUISITE for the field-ladder half of
// the destringify programme, and it inverts that programme's assumed order. Widening the
// ladder — so that inline and narrowed literal unions reach the atom rep the way aliased ones
// already do — recruits MORE programs into this defect, making it a net regression until
// these sinks widen. That was built, measured, reviewed and closed unmerged as #1536; see
// docs/internals/destringify-types-program.md B43.
//
// The alias spelling `K` is deliberate: it is the ONE spelling that reaches the field atom
// rep on master today, so this file pins the defect at its CURRENT reach rather than at the
// wider reach the ladder change would have given it.
type K = "ka" | "kb"
type R = { f: K }
function main() {
  const r: R = { f: "ka" }
  const xs: string[] = []
  xs.push(r.f)
  print(xs[0])
}
main()
